WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.858
Welcome to Module Nine, Lesson Two: prompt injection and agent security.

2
00:00:05.188 --> 00:00:10.194
It's the defining agent threat, because agents read untrusted content and then act.

3
00:00:10.994 --> 00:00:15.616
Picture a team whose coding agent reads code, runs tests, and pushes fixes.

4
00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:17.923
Its shell holds the team's cloud keys.

5
00:00:18.723 --> 00:00:20.977
One day it pulls in an open-source package.

6
00:00:21.307 --> 00:00:25.630
Buried in a code comment is a note written for AI assistants, not people.

7
00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.793
The agent obeys it and sends the keys to an outside server.

8
00:00:30.123 --> 00:00:32.121
Nobody on the team did anything wrong.

9
00:00:32.921 --> 00:00:36.818
That's indirect prompt injection, hidden in content the agent reads.

10
00:00:37.148 --> 00:00:39.598
Unlike direct injection from the user,

11
00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:43.413
it hides in documents, web pages, tool results, or memory.

12
00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:47.000
There's no complete fix, only layered containment.

13
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:51.120
First, treat all non-user content as data, never instructions.

14
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.639
Next, least privilege: only the tools it needs,

15
00:00:54.689 --> 00:00:56.819
with narrow, short-lived credentials.

16
00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:00.214
An agent that never holds the cloud keys can't leak them.

17
00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:04.484
Authorise every call server-side, under the user's identity.

18
00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:08.083
A hijacked agent then can't exceed that user's rights,

19
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:09.927
defeating the confused deputy.

20
00:01:10.727 --> 00:01:14.495
Require human approval for irreversible, high-impact actions.

21
00:01:14.825 --> 00:01:17.229
It holds even when everything upstream fails.

22
00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:22.587
Rails screen inputs and retrieved content for injection, and outputs for leaks.

23
00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:25.661
Egress control limits which hosts tools can reach.

24
00:01:26.461 --> 00:01:28.566
Finally, monitor tool sequences.

25
00:01:28.896 --> 00:01:32.601
A sudden network call right after reading a package is a red flag.

26
00:01:33.401 --> 00:01:37.319
Excessive agency means too many permissions, so split read from write.

27
00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:41.225
And never pass tool output into a shell or query unsanitised.

28
00:01:42.025 --> 00:01:46.541
Agent-written code runs in a sandbox, like a container or microVM.

29
00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:50.661
No ambient credentials, capped resources, and restricted network.

30
00:01:51.461 --> 00:01:55.528
Denial of wallet drives costly loops, so set rate limits and budgets.

31
00:01:55.858 --> 00:02:00.992
Memory poisoning fires later, so sanitise on write and distrust memory on read.

32
00:02:01.792 --> 00:02:02.617
The exam trap:

33
00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:07.054
a system-prompt line telling the model to ignore injected instructions.

34
00:02:07.384 --> 00:02:09.871
Prompts are defaults attackers can talk over,

35
00:02:09.921 --> 00:02:12.070
so enforce in the runtime, and read on.
